[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1843","stub":{"id":"1843","section":"01. Joe's Valley Reservoir to Route 29 (Straight Canyon)","altname":"Lower Seeley Creek","river":"Cottonwood Creek (Joes)","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-UTA"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Most of us are using the dirt pull out before the Dam service road. It is a short scramble to the river with a good eddy to wait for your crew to get in the water.","id":"108436","location":{"latitude":"39.287879","longitude":"-111.246741"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":"jort2wQ0ISsjuisH5faZD","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"The Birth Canal, This is the opening rapid. There are two lines, the first line, staying left line up to miss the first rock, at that rock turn to the right and bounce down the small drops and make the small opening between the two gateway rocks.\n\nThe second line is river right of the island, ducking a tree , hugging left, the water here is hitting a huge rock creating a pillow, staying left over the next couple small drops and making a right turn to go through the gateway.  At low flows I like the right line, less scraping. at higher flows the left line seems better.","id":"R7JnzOtmgbjIfjqGF8jHu","location":{"latitude":"39.285953","longitude":"-111.238488"},"name":"Birth Canal","distance":"0.49","photoID":"TriFTVSNf0L8mMk2wnyA9","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"S-Turn Rapid, This is just like the name says, it is an S-turn. At lower flows. 250-300 it is pretty straight forward, Watch your edges with the switching currents, it spins a lot of people around and they finish the rapid backwards.\n\nAt Higher flows 300+  a Sieve forms on the right side. So Stay left away from the Right rocks, On one of the trips we had two seperate individuals get stuck in there.","id":"tjgw4yn6ek26w141UldB0","location":{"latitude":"39.278682","longitude":"-111.228075"},"name":"S-Turn","distance":"1.25","photoID":"VPcQXKibFPLZm5YzmYs5w","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"The river here turns to the left, it pillows into a rock and creates an strong eddy on the right. Some may want to go into the eddy. Getting out of the eddy is very tricky. This is also a spot for wood to gather. Have had people in the group stuck in the eddy before, and there is a small rock that blocks the bigger rock that hangs people up on the corner if hit wrong. I don't believe it is a sieve though.","id":"FLBecegh5dR4aGKOIPxSJ","location":{"latitude":"39.274289","longitude":"-111.219483"},"name":"Mikes Eddy","distance":"1.83","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"xy9d28JS5O9A3JKtXtnT9"},{"type":"rapid","description":"Mine Cart,  in the  parking lot to scout this rapid is an old mining cart, hence the name. this starts with a significant drop, then numerous boulders to avoid. as you make your way around several corners. There are eddies to gather your thoughts, before continuing through. It is a fairly long section.","id":"69CoDjcXyFfJc87uMfxgS","location":{"latitude":"39.274359","longitude":"-111.206665"},"name":"Mine Cart Rapid","distance":"2.54","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":"weObskQBXtDcGwby4tv7t"},{"type":"takeout","description":"This is an alternate take out, due to the restroom and large parking lot.","id":"ZQYXavOLsmywMYBassVib","location":{"latitude":"39.272783","longitude":"-111.194698"},"name":"Halloween Town Parking (Alt take out) ","distance":"3.23","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"This is a Diversion Dam, It has been run, good chance there could be wood, it is very short maybe 6' tall.","id":"nBkiz8iSoaNl5Kf8alQsS","location":{"latitude":"39.274587","longitude":"-111.186636"},"name":"Diversion Drop","distance":"3.7","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"Paddle puller, this rapid wraps around an island the inside seems to be a little less manky. And how fitting is the name, the outside corner has taken a paddle from someone in the group everytime I have run it. It is a short drop with some boulder dodging.","id":"D7kVf8tcjerRfuWzUDwEm","location":{"latitude":"39.27581","longitude":"-111.1807"},"name":"Paddle Puller","distance":"4.04","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"The last drop before the take out. A nice little gem of a suprise, stay on your toes, this one can get you.","id":"IHNADertGpQiPcOjtzwLB","location":{"latitude":"39.269693","longitude":"-111.151122"},"name":"Hidden Treasure","distance":"5.89","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"Exit rapid is a nice  little boulder garden. Be careful there is barb wire down stream of here. So watch out for it!","id":"pWTbnmGxEb3acoZMH1F6z","location":{"latitude":"39.266292","longitude":"-111.137006"},"name":"Exit rapid","distance":"6.76","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"113671","location":{"latitude":"39.260755","longitude":"-111.119779"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"7.97","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"893614","ext":"jpg","post_id":"897154","description":null,"caption":"Cottonwood Creek @01. Joe's Valley Reservoir to Route 29 (Straight Canyon)","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893614","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893614"}},{"id":"jort2wQ0ISsjuisH5faZD","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"The short scramble to the put it. ","caption":"Put in ","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":"108436","photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/jort2wQ0ISsjuisH5faZD","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/jort2wQ0ISsjuisH5faZD"}},{"id":"11065","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6775","description":"This eddy often suckers paddlers.  It drains pretty powerfully under the log and rock. The rock visible in the main current flips some paddlers.  Immediately downstream is a log jam on the left 2/3 of the river.  Avoid swimming.","caption":"Will's Angry Eddy","poi_name":"","subject":"log/sieve on Cottonwood Cr.","author":"epc","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11065","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11065"}},{"id":"xy9d28JS5O9A3JKtXtnT9","ext":"jpg","post_id":"9Wk8NeNtZP15gFH4oIkEs","description":"Easily seen from road and river if you know what to look for. As we left the eddy above the rock on the left, our friend did not make the ferry and got caught in it. ","caption":"Sieve","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Keenan Peters","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-04-26T22:36:05.000Z","updated_at":"2023-04-26T22:36:05.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/xy9d28JS5O9A3JKtXtnT9","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/xy9d28JS5O9A3JKtXtnT9"}},{"id":"VPcQXKibFPLZm5YzmYs5w","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"At higher flows 300+ a Sieve forms on the right side. Most of the water will try and push you into it. ","caption":"S-turn","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":"tjgw4yn6ek26w141UldB0","photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/VPcQXKibFPLZm5YzmYs5w","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/VPcQXKibFPLZm5YzmYs5w"}},{"id":"SjLkeCWpxtA0tPcgA16LF","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/SjLkeCWpxtA0tPcgA16LF","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/SjLkeCWpxtA0tPcgA16LF"}},{"id":"CpnKbXG6EQ4xuFiOdyzWM","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"","caption":"Alt Take out","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":"ZQYXavOLsmywMYBassVib","photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/CpnKbXG6EQ4xuFiOdyzWM","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/CpnKbXG6EQ4xuFiOdyzWM"}},{"id":"TriFTVSNf0L8mMk2wnyA9","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"The Birth Canal, This is the opening rapid. There are two lines, the first line, staying left line up to miss the first rock, at that rock turn to the right and bounce down the small drops and make the small opening between the two gateway rocks. \nThe second line is river right of the island, ducking a tree , hugging left, the water here is hitting a huge rock creating a pillow, staying left over the next couple small drops and making a right turn to go through the gateway.  At low flows I like the right line, less scraping. at higher flows the left line seems better. ","caption":"Birth Canal","poi_name":null,"subject":"Rapid","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":"R7JnzOtmgbjIfjqGF8jHu","photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/TriFTVSNf0L8mMk2wnyA9","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/TriFTVSNf0L8mMk2wnyA9"}},{"id":"qoweGxN529ltQtVCwGLLT","ext":"jpg","post_id":"9Wk8NeNtZP15gFH4oIkEs","description":"Easy to see from the road","caption":"Tree across River.","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Keenan Peters","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-04-26T22:36:05.000Z","updated_at":"2023-04-26T22:36:05.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/qoweGxN529ltQtVCwGLLT","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/qoweGxN529ltQtVCwGLLT"}},{"id":"weObskQBXtDcGwby4tv7t","ext":"jpg","post_id":"oGfUpoGvYyEsm9NgSV0IG","description":"Chad Phillips has made the first drop into Minecart, Liam Redmond is setting up in the eddy above. ","caption":"Mine Cart","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Cody Redmond","poi_id":"69CoDjcXyFfJc87uMfxgS","photo_date":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-25T19:09:45.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/weObskQBXtDcGwby4tv7t","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/weObskQBXtDcGwby4tv7t"}}],"detail":{"id":"1843","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Cottonwood Creek, more appropriately named Lower Seeley Creek (or simply 'Joe's'), flows from Joes Valley Reservoir. The same turquoise water that attracts boaters and fisherman to this man made lake is found in the river. It really is a beautiful stretch of desert paddling when it has water. Most seasons don't see many releases over 200 cfs. While Gary Nichols states a level of 350+ or more is desirable for this section, it has been run as low as 180 cfs. While the creek isnt as pushy at these lower flows, there are more spots where one could pin a boat.\n\nThe rapids are generally pool drop in nature and are formed where large boulders have cleaved from the canyon walls and dropped to the stream bed. Two drops standout in my mind. The first consists of a left hand bend with three paths dropping approximately 4 feet into a pool below. This pool drains out a narrow slot just left of center and probably underneath the river right boulder. The second drop comes after a few class III-ish drops and is marked by a large boulder (house size) on the left hand bank. I frequently encounter trees pinned in here so a scout is advisable. The action continues to the take out with a number of less steep drops and boulder gardens. Our usual takeout is a dirt pullout on the road just after the canyon begins to open up. (Driving up the canyon, this is the only pull-off that leaves the road after the junction with Cottonwood canyon and Straight Canyon.) One may put-in anywhere along the road, some popular options are the last pull-out by the large Pine tree before the road climbs the grade up to the top of the dam, or by walking down the lower dam access road and bush-whacking to the river. (Please don't park on the dam access road, or anywhere blocking the main highway. After 9/11, the dam itself is a restricted area, please obey the no tresspassing signs)\n\nThere's plenty of free or National Forest camping around Joes Valley. Check some of the dirt roads leading north or west from the reservoir for some nice secluded spots. (Camping is no longer allowed at the take-out). There are the standard pit toilets located off the main road on the northwest edge of the reservoir. There is a liquor store in Castle Dale these days, at 160 E Main St.\n\nFlows:\n\nWhen the reservoir is spilling, in 2024 there was a 'Total Water Outflow' number here, in 2025 its no longer there. In 2025 we see Pipeline Outflow.\n\n<http://www.ewcd.org/reservoirs/joes-valley-reservoir/>\n\nHere is a link with a more complete-sounding 'Joes Valley Reservoir Discharge'.\n\n[http://www.ewcd.org/canals/cottonwood/](http://www.ewcd.org/canals/cottonwood/?fbclid=IwZXh0bgNhZW0CMTAAYnJpZBExUFdYeUJqVzhCWmZrUmFRSwEe_TIZfgAdnKw7U4KKVoPswHBI5TCjpvkwliRD8ZzljSF3UBlUKaT1rtewZmM_aem_0Xq3U_OXyARpU8LZL58sUA)\n\n\n\nInflow, outflow, and reservoir storage forecasts:\n\n<https://www.cbrfc.noaa.gov/station/flowplot/flowplot.cgi?JOVU1>","averageGradient":"90","maxGradient":"130","length":"8","correlations":[],"pointOfIntere